data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

**Sync I/O on the Cosmos DB provider is fully removed** — no opt-in, no workaround. In .NET 10 you could set the sync-over-async flag; in EF Core 11 all synchronous calls **always throw**.

Your `GetAll`, `GetById`, `GetCount`, and `Save` methods all use affected APIs. Convert them to async:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Mapping:** `ToList()` → `ToListAsync()`, `First()` → `FirstAsync()`, `Count()` → `CountAsync()`, `SaveChanges()` → `SaveChangesAsync()`.

All callers must also become `async`/`await`. There is no way to keep synchronous Cosmos DB access in EF Core 11.

**Bonus:** You also have a second Cosmos-related change — owned collections with no items now return an **empty collection** instead of `null`. Update any `is null` checks to `.Count == 0` if applicable.
